+LIM-11. While X0X follows an external clock, it sends no Clock, Start or Stop (CLOCK OUT is muted), so no clock loop occurs. A MIDI Start that arrives when no clock came in the last 0.5 s starts X0X on its internal clock. CLOCK OUT is on by default (SET-08). In clip mode, X0X does not echo a MIDI Start, Continue or Stop (seq midi_req). Until the first incoming tick it can still send internal clock ticks (0xF8) to the USB host. With clip mode off, X0X sends Start (0xFA) and clock ticks back to the USB host until the first incoming tick, as before (agent-rules 10). [CODE app/engine.c:469-472] [CODE seq/sequencer.c:378-379] [CODE seq/sequencer.c:392-393] [CODE seq/sequencer.c:470] [CODE seq/sequencer.c:446-455] [CODE seq/sequencer.c:511-512] If the clock then starts while X0X plays, the first tick is tick 0 of the step that plays. The next step comes 6 ticks later (16ths). (Changed 2026-10-16, a72: before, the first tick played the next step at once, so X0X played one step ahead of the clock.) [CODE seq/sequencer.c seq_advance]
 LIM-13. Bluetooth MIDI (BLE-01 to BLE-10) is not tested on hardware yet (Stage 3: docs/plans/2026-10-15-ble-stage3-test-plan.md). The RF calibration values are the AC79 SDK demo board values, not the unit's own. The clocks given to the image (system 360 MHz, HSB 180 MHz, LSB 60 MHz) were measured on an FM-1, not on an SMK-37. [CODE ../hal/fm1_ble.h]
 LIM-14. The controller's poll and its interrupts run on core 1 between and inside audio jobs. A long poll can delay an audio job past the 8 ms limit, and then X0X holds core 1 (one core, no Bluetooth). Long flash operations park core 1, and a long park can drop the BLE connection. Units that cannot run core 1 (fm1-x0x#10) have no Bluetooth. [CODE ../hal/fm1_cpu1.h]
 LIM-12. Only the SMK-37 build is maintained. The FM-1 build is not maintained. Its link step fails because the XIP region overflows by about 648 B. The user accepts this. It is not a defect. [USER]
